#: ../../../source/api/ABQflow.rst:26
msgid "ABQflow.datkit module"
msgstr ""

#: ABQflow.datkit:1 of
msgid "datkit — parser for Abaqus ``.dat`` printed output."
msgstr ""

#: ABQflow.datkit:3 of
msgid ""
"Single file, stdlib only, Py2.7 / Py3 compatible — the same contract as "
":mod:`ABQflow.hookkit`, and for the same reason: this file is staged into"
" the job directory and imported by hook scripts, which may run under an "
"interpreter that is not the one ABQflow itself runs on.  It never imports"
" ABQflow."
msgstr ""

#: ABQflow.datkit:9 of
msgid "Why this exists"
msgstr ""

#: ABQflow.datkit:10 of
msgid ""
"An ``.odb`` is often too large to open just to read a handful of numbers."
"  The usual workaround is to add ``*EL PRINT`` / ``*NODE PRINT`` to the "
"deck so that Abaqus also writes the values as text into ``<job>.dat``.  "
"This module turns that text back into rows and columns."
msgstr ""

#: ABQflow.datkit:16 of
msgid "What it parses"
msgstr ""

#: ABQflow.datkit:17 of
#, python-format
msgid ""
"The ``.dat`` is ~99% input echo followed by, per converged increment, an "
"``INCREMENT n SUMMARY`` block and one printed table per output request.  "
"A table is a banner (``N O D E   O U T P U T``), an optional \"THE "
"FOLLOWING TABLE IS PRINTED FOR ...\" line naming the set, a two-line "
"header whose ``FOOT-`` / ``NOTE`` heading is split across both lines, the"
" data rows, and ``MAXIMUM`` / ``MINIMUM`` / ``AT NODE`` summary rows.  "
"Those land in ``table['summary']``, where the ``AT NODE`` rows are keyed "
"``'MAXIMUM AT'`` / ``'MINIMUM AT'`` after the row each one annotates."
msgstr ""

#: ABQflow.datkit:26 ABQflow.hookkit.opened:3 of
msgid "Usage::"
msgstr ""

#: ABQflow.datkit:35 of
msgid "For a file too large to hold, or when only one increment is wanted::"
msgstr ""

#: ABQflow.datkit:42 of
msgid ""
"The Py2.7 half of the promise is load-bearing — no f-strings, no "
"annotations, no ``open(..., encoding=...)``, every class explicitly "
"``(object)``.  ``test/unit/test_hookkit_py27.py`` enforces it with an AST"
" scan over this file as well as hookkit.py; run it before changing "
"anything here."
msgstr ""

#: ABQflow.datkit.to_float:1 of
msgid "Parse one printed value, returning ``None`` rather than raising."
msgstr ""

#: ABQflow.datkit.to_float:3 of
msgid ""
"Handles plain floats, Fortran ``D`` exponents (``1.0D-03``), the "
"missing-``E`` three-digit exponent form (``4.2961278-303``), ``***`` "
"overflow fields, and NaN/Inf — all of which appear in real ``.dat`` files"
" and none of which should kill a whole table."
msgstr ""

#: ABQflow.datkit.match_set:1 of
msgid "Case-insensitive :mod:`fnmatch` of a table's set name against *pattern*."
msgstr ""

#: ABQflow.datkit.match_set:3 of
msgid ""
"``.dat`` upper-cases set names relative to the INP, so an exact name from"
" the deck still matches.  ``None`` *pattern* matches everything; a table "
"with no set never matches a non-``None`` pattern."
msgstr ""

#: ABQflow.datkit.iter_tables:1 of
msgid "Yield tables one at a time, retaining nothing between them."
msgstr ""

#: ABQflow.datkit.iter_tables:3 of
msgid ""
"Use this instead of :func:`parse` when the file is large and the caller "
"only needs to fold over the tables.  *steps* accepts an int or a sequence"
" of ints — ``'last'`` cannot be resolved while streaming, so ask "
":func:`parse` for that."
msgstr ""

#: ABQflow.datkit.parse:1 of
msgid "Parse *path* into a document dict."
msgstr ""

