#: ABQflow.datkit.to_rows:15 of
msgid "include_labels"
msgstr ""

#: ABQflow.datkit.to_rows:16 of
msgid "Prepend the label columns (``NODE``, or ``ELEMENT`` + ``PT``)."
msgstr ""

#: ABQflow.datkit.to_rows:17 of
msgid "include_footnote"
msgstr ""

#: ABQflow.datkit.to_rows:18 of
msgid ""
"Keep the footnote column.  Off by default: it holds text, and "
":func:`ABQflow.helpers.convert.load_field` expects every cell of a field "
"to be numeric."
msgstr ""

#: ABQflow.datkit.column_values:1 of
msgid "Every value of one column of one table, in row order."
msgstr ""

#: ABQflow.datkit.summary:1 of
#, python-brace-format
msgid "One summary row of *table* as a ``{column: value}`` mapping."
msgstr ""

#: ABQflow.datkit.summary:3 of
msgid ""
"*row* is ``'MAXIMUM'``, ``'MINIMUM'``, ``'TOTAL'``, ``'AVERAGE'``, ... or"
" one of the label rows: Abaqus prints ``AT NODE`` (or ``AT ELEMENT``) "
"twice, once under ``MAXIMUM`` and once under ``MINIMUM``, so each is "
"keyed by the row it belongs to — ``'MAXIMUM AT'`` and ``'MINIMUM AT'`` — "
"and asking for ``'MAXIMUM AT'`` tells you *where* each column peaked."
msgstr ""

#: ABQflow.datkit.summary:9 of
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Returns an :class:`~collections.OrderedDict` so callers get a stable "
"column order under Python 2.7 as well.  An unknown *row* gives ``{}``."
msgstr ""

#: ABQflow.datkit.reduce_values:1 of
msgid "Reduce a list of numbers to one."
msgstr ""

#: ABQflow.datkit.reduce_values:3 of
msgid ""
"``'absmax'`` / ``'absmin'`` pick by magnitude but return the value with "
"its **sign intact** — a Riks analysis that snaps back drives the response"
" negative, and the peak of that curve is a negative number."
msgstr ""

#: ABQflow.datkit.reduce_rows:1 of
msgid "Reduce one named column of a ``(header, rows)`` pair to a scalar."
